{
  "image_name": "25c4b30331ca6c71f640d6fe291d7dec.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure sketches a photophysical pathway linking an excited singlet state to coupled triplet–radical spin states. Light absorption promotes the molecule from the ground singlet \\(S_0\\) to an excited singlet \\(S_1\\), shown as a \\(\\pi \\rightarrow \\pi^*\\) excitation. From there, an EISC step converts the system into a triplet excited state \\(T\\) together with a radical doublet \\(D_0\\).\n\nThe middle of the figure emphasizes how these spin manifolds change from zero field to high field. The triplet splits into three sublevels, labeled roughly \\(T_+\\), \\(T_0\\), and \\(T_-\\), while the radical doublet splits into \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) spin states. These separate levels are then brought together by spin coupling.\n\nOn the right, coupling between the triplet and doublet produces two overall spin manifolds: a doublet excited state \\(D_1\\) and a quartet state \\(Q\\). Their level structures are shown in terms of total spin projections, with \\(D_1\\) having \\(\\pm 1/2\\) and \\(Q\\) having \\(\\pm 3/2\\) and \\(\\pm 1/2\\). A red arrow labeled \\(3J_{TR}\\) marks the exchange-energy gap between the doublet and quartet manifolds. Overall, the diagram explains how photoexcitation, intersystem crossing, magnetic-field splitting, and triplet–radical coupling generate distinct spin states.",
